<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izates II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izates II was king of Adiabene in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 1st century CE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, he and his mother Queen Helena converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. He helped the Parthian king Artabanus II regain his throne and was rewarded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, and he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the tomb built by his mother.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izates-ii.jpg" alt="Izates II" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Adiabene,_ca_37_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Izates bar Monobaz, born about 1 CE, was the son of Monobaz I of Adiabene and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helena-of-adiabene">Helena of Adiabene</a>, who was also said to be the wife of King Abgarus of Edessa. In his youth his father sent him to the court of King Abinergaos I of Characene at Charax Spasinu. There a Jewish merchant named Ananias taught him the tenets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, in which he became deeply interested, and he married the king's daughter Symacho, whom Ananias had also brought to the Jewish faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Izates came to the throne on his father's death, around 30 or 31, and found that his mother had meanwhile become a Jew without his knowledge. He wished to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> fully, including circumcision; Ananias and Helena talked him out of it, but another Jew, Eleazar, later persuaded him. The midrash Genesis Rabbah tells the story another way: Izates and his brother Monobaz, reading the verse of Genesis on circumcision, each wept and then went in secret to be circumcised.</p><p>For a time he reigned in peace and was so respected that he was chosen around 39 to arbitrate between the Parthian king Artabanus II and his rebellious nobles. When several of his relatives openly declared their conversion, nobles of Adiabene secretly urged Abia, an Arab king, to make war on him; Izates defeated him, and Abia took his own life in despair. The nobles then conspired with Vologases, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, who was stopped at the last moment from carrying out his plans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Izates died around 54 after a reign of twenty-four years, and his mother survived him only a short time. He left twenty-four sons and twenty-four daughters. He was succeeded by his older brother Monobaz II, who sent the remains of Izates and of Queen Helena to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for burial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Izates II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Josephus tells how Queen Helena and her son King <b>Izates II</b> adopted Judaism in the 1st century CE; Helena sent food to Jerusalem during a famine and was buried in the so-called Tomb of the Kings there, and Adiabenian princes fought for the Jews against Rome in 66…</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-adiabene">Kingdom of Adiabene</a></li></ul></section>
